import{_ as t,o as a,c as r,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.Ci_RTJJ5.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Where next","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"chapters/where-next.md","filePath":"chapters/where-next.md"}'),o={name:"chapters/where-next.md"};function d(n,e,i,h,l,c){return a(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="where-next" tabindex="-1">Where next <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-next" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where next&quot;">​</a></h1><p>You have an agent that talks to a model, runs tools, remembers a session, holds a context budget, accepts steering, reads skills, delegates to a subagent, and calls tools that live in another process.</p><p>This page lists what to build on top of it. Every entry names the seam it attaches to and the claim that proves it works. A claim you cannot test is a wish.</p><h2 id="the-rule" tabindex="-1">The rule <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-rule" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The rule&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Write the case first.</p><p>A feature with no failing case is indistinguishable from a feature that is broken. Every chapter in this course arrived with a suite that failed before the code existed. Keep doing that and the seams will keep holding.</p><h2 id="next-week" tabindex="-1">Next week <a class="header-anchor" href="#next-week" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Next week&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Six changes, each a day&#39;s work, each attaching to a seam you already have.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Build</th><th>Seam</th><th>Claim</th><th>Size</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Recall what was pruned</td><td>new <code>Tool</code> plus the budget decorator</td><td>a pruned message can be fetched by id</td><td>small</td></tr><tr><td>Summarise instead of dropping</td><td>budget decorator</td><td>the request is under budget and contains one summary entry naming the count</td><td>medium</td></tr><tr><td>A schema budget</td><td>budget decorator</td><td>no request advertises more than N tools</td><td>small</td></tr><tr><td>Cache accounting</td><td>provider decorator</td><td>the request prefix is byte-identical across turns, and hits are reported</td><td>small</td></tr><tr><td>Parallel tool calls</td><td>loop</td><td>two calls run concurrently and results keep source order</td><td>medium</td></tr><tr><td>A second wire format</td><td><code>Provider</code></td><td>the same suite passes against two providers</td><td>medium</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="recall-what-was-pruned" tabindex="-1">Recall what was pruned <a class="header-anchor" href="#recall-what-was-pruned" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Recall what was pruned&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Chapter 07 drops old messages and leaves a marker. The content is still in the session file, so make it reachable: give every dropped message an id, and add a <code>recall</code> tool that returns one by id.</p><p>The claim: with a pruned session, a <code>recall</code> call returns text that was absent from the request. This is what pi-condense does with <code>context_tree_query</code>, and it turns pruning from lossy into reversible.</p><h3 id="summarise-instead-of-dropping" tabindex="-1">Summarise instead of dropping <a class="header-anchor" href="#summarise-instead-of-dropping" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Summarise instead of dropping&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Instead of a marker, insert a summary message that lists what was removed.</p><p>The claim: after compaction the budget holds, and the request contains exactly one summary entry naming how many messages it replaces. The hard part is that a summary needs a model call, which means the thing that trims the request now makes requests. That is a loop, so decide where it lives before you write it.</p><h3 id="a-schema-budget" tabindex="-1">A schema budget <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-schema-budget" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A schema budget&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Chapter 07 counts tool schemas but never reduces them. Add a ceiling on how many tools a request may advertise, and unmount the least recently used until it fits.</p><p>The claim: with 20 tools registered and a ceiling of 5, the request advertises 5, and a call to an unmounted tool returns a result that says so instead of crashing.</p><p>Two designs exist. Dropping tools is simple. Deferring them, where one meta-tool loads another tool&#39;s schema on demand, is what fx does, and it is the better design for a large catalogue.</p><h3 id="cache-accounting" tabindex="-1">Cache accounting <a class="header-anchor" href="#cache-accounting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cache accounting&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Providers charge less when the start of a request is unchanged. You already keep the system message stable. Now measure it: hash the serialised prefix on each request, report whether it matched the previous one, and accumulate prompt tokens against cache hits and misses.</p><p>The claim: two turns in a row produce the same prefix hash, and the reported hit count increases.</p><h3 id="parallel-tool-calls" tabindex="-1">Parallel tool calls <a class="header-anchor" href="#parallel-tool-calls" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Parallel tool calls&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Chapter 04 runs a tool batch one call at a time. Run them concurrently instead, and append the results in the order the model asked for them.</p><p>The claim: two calls take roughly one call&#39;s wall time, and the result order still matches the call order. The second half is the part that breaks, because completion order is not source order.</p><h3 id="a-second-wire-format" tabindex="-1">A second wire format <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-second-wire-format" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A second wire format&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Add Anthropic&#39;s Messages API beside the OpenAI-compatible one.</p><p>The claim: the chapter 01 to 03 suites pass against both providers without changing a case. If they do not, the provider seam was leaky, and you have found out cheaply.</p><h2 id="later" tabindex="-1">Later <a class="header-anchor" href="#later" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Later&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Bigger changes. Each one alters a structure rather than adding to it.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Build</th><th>Seam</th><th>What changes</th><th>Size</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sessions as a tree</td><td><code>Recorder</code></td><td>a message points at its parent; branching and forking become possible</td><td>large</td></tr><tr><td>Background subagents</td><td><code>ToolSource</code> plus a queue</td><td>a child returns a receipt and delivers later</td><td>large</td></tr><tr><td>MCP resources and prompts</td><td>MCP client</td><td>the protocol grows past <code>tools/</code></td><td>medium</td></tr><tr><td>A terminal UI</td><td>event stream</td><td>the events you emit become a rendered transcript</td><td>large</td></tr><tr><td>An extension API</td><td>every seam</td><td>in-process plugins register tools and contributors</td><td>large</td></tr><tr><td>Evals</td><td>nothing</td><td>a second harness that scores outcomes, not correctness</td><td>large</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="sessions-as-a-tree" tabindex="-1">Sessions as a tree <a class="header-anchor" href="#sessions-as-a-tree" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sessions as a tree&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Today a session is a line. Make each record point at a parent, and the file becomes a tree: branch from any point, fork a conversation, keep a compaction beside the original instead of over it.</p><p>The claim: forking at message 5 produces a session whose request contains messages 1 to 5 and then the new turn, and the original file still reads back unchanged.</p><p>pi&#39;s session format is the reference. A line-shaped file cannot express this, so the change is structural, not additive.</p><h3 id="background-subagents" tabindex="-1">Background subagents <a class="header-anchor" href="#background-subagents" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Background subagents&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Chapter 10 blocks the parent while the child works. The alternative returns a receipt immediately and delivers the answer later as a separate message.</p><p>The claim: a parent that starts a child and then answers something else receives the child&#39;s result in a later turn, exactly once, without a second <code>tool_result</code> for the original call.</p><p>The hard parts are ordering and cancellation: a result can arrive when no call is waiting, and a parent can be interrupted while its child is still running.</p><h3 id="mcp-resources-and-prompts" tabindex="-1">MCP resources and prompts <a class="header-anchor" href="#mcp-resources-and-prompts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;MCP resources and prompts&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Your client speaks <code>tools/</code>. MCP also defines resources (readable documents) and prompts (reusable templates). Add <code>resources/list</code> and <code>resources/read</code>, expose them through a skill-like contributor, and keep the tool adapter untouched.</p><p>The claim: a resource appears in the system prompt by name, and reading it returns the server&#39;s text.</p><h3 id="a-terminal-ui" tabindex="-1">A terminal UI <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-terminal-ui" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A terminal UI&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The event stream is already the right shape: <code>turn_start</code>, <code>text</code>, <code>tool_call</code>, <code>tool_result</code>, <code>turn_end</code>, <code>budget</code>, <code>steer</code>, <code>agent_end</code>. A UI is a renderer over those events plus a way to type steering messages.</p><p>Bubble Tea is the Go default. The claim that matters: the same events render to a terminal and to a text log, so the UI cannot contain logic the log does not have.</p><h3 id="an-extension-api" tabindex="-1">An extension API <a class="header-anchor" href="#an-extension-api" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;An extension API&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Every chapter added a seam. An extension API turns those seams into a public surface: a plugin registers tools, contributors, and event handlers, and receives the registry and the provider.</p><p>This is what pi ships, and the honest version is a large design. Read how pi&#39;s <code>AgentLoopConfig</code> exposes hooks before you invent one.</p><h3 id="evals" tabindex="-1">Evals <a class="header-anchor" href="#evals" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Evals&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Everything in this course grades correctness: did the request have the right shape, did the result pair with its call. Nothing grades quality: was the answer any good.</p><p>Evals need a real model, money, and judgement, and they cannot gate a run. Build them as a separate tool that scores a transcript after the fact, and never let them decide whether the harness works.</p><h2 id="what-not-to-build" tabindex="-1">What not to build <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-not-to-build" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What not to build&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>A second pi.</strong> Feature parity is not a goal. The machine is.</li><li><strong>A feature whose only evidence is that the program still runs.</strong> That is what a case is for.</li><li><strong>A live-model check inside the graded suite.</strong> It is slow, paid, and random. Keep it outside, clearly labelled, and never let it gate progress.</li><li><strong>An abstraction before the second use.</strong> Every seam in this course appeared when two chapters needed it, not before.</li></ul><h2 id="read-these" tabindex="-1">Read these <a class="header-anchor" href="#read-these" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Read these&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Concern</th><th>File</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Loop shape, event order, result batching</td><td>PiG <code>agent/agent_loop.go</code></td></tr><tr><td>Provider adapters, streaming, caching</td><td>pi <code>packages/ai/src/api/</code></td></tr><tr><td>Permission policy as a service</td><td>crush <code>internal/permission/</code></td></tr><tr><td>MCP over stdio, hand-rolled</td><td>PiG and pi MCP clients</td></tr><tr><td>Session tree and compaction entries</td><td>pi <code>packages/durable/</code>, <code>docs/compaction.md</code></td></tr><tr><td>Pruning with recovery</td><td>pi-condense, <code>context_tree_query</code></td></tr><tr><td>Deferred tool schemas</td><td>toolflow, stage-gated mounting</td></tr><tr><td>A minimal harness in Zig</td><td><code>vercel-labs/fx</code></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="one-last-thing" tabindex="-1">One last thing <a class="header-anchor" href="#one-last-thing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;One last thing&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The harness is not the interesting part. The interesting part is that you can now read a harness and tell what it does, because you have built one and watched it fail in specific, named ways.</p><p>That is what the cases bought you.</p>',58)])])}const m=t(o,[["render",d]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
